[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says one bug: the parser reads dates month first. The reports in it show
three problems. Each one now has its own ticket, so a fix for one is not taken as a fix for all.

<Callout kind="warn">
The queued parser fix closes the <code>parser</code> problem only. <code>hint</code> and
<code>one-day</code> stay broken after it ships.
</Callout>

## The problems in #4471

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} tickets={TICKETS} />

### `parser`: the shared parser reads dates month first

A day-first tenant's date comes out with day and month swapped: "Booked for 2 December. The invite says
12 February." This is what #4471 describes. It covers every report from a day-first tenant that came in
by app or email, and form reports from tenants on `booking-v2`. Fix: parse in the tenant's locale.

### `hint`: the `booking-v3` form tells day-first people to type month first

`booking-v3` prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. People follow the hint, and the form reads what
they typed the tenant's way, day first. The swap looks the same as `parser`, but it comes from the form,
so the parser fix does not touch it. It covers form reports from the day-first tenants on `booking-v3`.
Fix: print the hint in the tenant's order, and read the field in that same order.

### `one-day`: bookings move by exactly one day

"Invite says Tuesday 12 May. Calendar says Monday 11 May." "Client in Lisbon got Tuesday. Client in
Toronto got Monday." A day–month swap cannot do this: it moves a date by months. These point at a time
zone or day-boundary fault: a date taken at midnight in one zone and shown in another. The desk had
already read them as one-day moves (`desk-notes.json`) and footnoted them under `parser`; here they are a
problem of their own. Fix: store a booking as a calendar date plus the tenant's time zone, not as a
midnight timestamp.

## How each report is placed

1. Count each report once: the export repeats some ids.
2. If the desk read it as a one-day move, it is `one-day`.
3. Otherwise `causes.ts` decides by tenant, not by intake's tag: a form report from a day-first tenant
   on `booking-v3` is `hint`; any other report from a day-first tenant is `parser`.

The logic is in `#lib/kb/problems.ts`, and the table runs it at render time, so the counts follow the
data as the desk keeps merging.

## Why #4471 held all three

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on subject alone, and all three problems
arrive with the same subjects: "Wrong day on the invite", "Date is wrong". Intake's tag guesses from the
reporter's browser and disagrees with the tenant's form often. Read what a report shows before you merge
it.

